From: Martin Beck <martin.beck2@gmx.de>
Date: Mon, 8 Jun 2026 15:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] docs: cover page-cache and flush saturation follow-ups

Record the follow-up all-core perf and iostat interpretation for the
bm_min_rocks_fcntl_pread64_0_0 benchmark.

The page-cache section explains why allocator-zero bypass is unsafe and
points validation toward ext4 buffered iomap with large-folio support.
The storage section documents fsync_batch_usec and the ordering rule
that allows only pre-submission callers to share a flush.

Signed-off-by: Martin Beck <martin.beck2@gmx.de>
---
 .../ext4/bm-min-rocks-optimizations.rst       | 60 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/filesystems/ext4/bm-min-rocks-optimizations.rst b/Documentation/filesystems/ext4/bm-min-rocks-optimizations.rst
index 1ca6f860aa4f..4e5491c9f248 100644
--- a/Documentation/filesystems/ext4/bm-min-rocks-optimizations.rst
+++ b/Documentation/filesystems/ext4/bm-min-rocks-optimizations.rst
@@ -68,12 +68,62 @@ two changes reduce work in that exact clean truncate path after the prior
 but removes repeated per-buffer accounting when folios contain multiple
 buffers.
 
+Follow-up full-system perf and iostat results added two more observations.
+First, with all CPUs sampled the largest leaf under the write path can be
+``clear_page_erms`` from page-cache folio allocation.  This is particularly
+visible on kernels built with ``CONFIG_INIT_ON_ALLOC_DEFAULT_ON=y``.  The safe
+kernel-side mitigation is not to bypass allocator zeroing: page-cache contents
+must never expose stale memory if a short copy, signal, mmap race, or partial
+write leaves bytes outside the user-supplied range.  Instead, this branch relies
+on ext4's buffered-iomap path with large-folio support, which amortizes
+allocation and zeroing over larger folios without weakening memory-disclosure
+hardening.  Benchmark runs intended to exercise this path should avoid the
+``nobuffered_iomap`` mount option and should confirm in perf that writes enter
+``ext4_iomap_buffered_write()``/``iomap_file_buffered_write()`` rather than the
+legacy ``ext4_da_write_begin()`` buffer-head path.
+
+Second, iostat shows that high parallelism drives the active NVMe device to
+roughly 2 GiB/s of writes, rising queue depth, and increasing write latency.
+The benchmark issues one ``fdatasync()`` per 2 MiB rewrite, so device cache
+flushes become an important scalability limiter.  For opt-in experiments, this
+branch adds an ext4 mount option:
+
+``fsync_batch_usec=N``
+  Hold a required fsync/fdatasync device flush in a short collection window of
+  up to ``N`` microseconds, capped at 10000.  Concurrent callers that have
+  already completed data writeback and journal commit before the flush is
+  submitted can share that one flush.  The default is 0, which preserves the
+  existing behavior.
+
+Flush batching correctness
+--------------------------
+
+``fsync_batch_usec`` only affects the final ``blkdev_issue_flush()`` step after
+``ext4_sync_file()`` has already run ``file_write_and_wait_range()`` and waited
+for the relevant journal transaction.  A caller can join an existing batch only
+while the batch leader is still collecting and has not submitted the flush.  A
+caller arriving after the flush has been submitted waits for it to finish and
+then starts a new batch, because that caller's data may be newer than the
+already-submitted flush.
+
+This preserves the essential fdatasync ordering guarantee: every caller served
+by a shared flush completed its data and metadata prerequisites before that
+flush began, and the syscall returns only after the shared flush completes.  The
+option trades a bounded amount of latency for fewer flush commands under
+massively parallel fsync-heavy workloads.  It should be left at the default
+``0`` for latency-sensitive deployments unless measurements justify batching.
+
 Current validation
 ------------------
 
-The code changes pass ``scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict`` for the three code
-commits and compile as individual objects with an x86_64 ``defconfig``:
+The code changes pass ``scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict``.  The fsync batching
+commit has one expected ``CAMELCASE`` check for the new ``Opt_*`` mount parser
+token, matching the surrounding ext4 option style; rerunning checkpatch with
+``--ignore CAMELCASE`` is clean.  The touched objects compile with an x86_64
+``defconfig``:
 
+* ``fs/ext4/fsync.o``
+* ``fs/ext4/super.o``
 * ``fs/ext4/inode.o``
 * ``fs/ext4/page-io.o``
 * ``fs/buffer.o``
@@ -88,3 +138,9 @@ maintainers.
 These patches should be validated with the same benchmark using full-system
 perf sampling, lock contention tracing around JBD2 and buffer-head locks, and
 xfstests coverage for ext4 ordered, writeback, and data-journal modes.
+Additional storage-saturation validation should compare ``fsync_batch_usec=0``
+against small values such as 50, 100, and 250 microseconds while tracking
+``iostat`` flush rate, write latency, queue depth, and benchmark throughput.
+Crash-consistency testing should include power-fail style tests around
+concurrent ``fdatasync()`` calls and remount testing that toggles
+``fsync_batch_usec``.
